Subject: Re: gbm, survival, risk score
Message-ID: <Ef1V6RWoX1A.i7QjDE9bBAAJ@gbm-dev.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/gbm-dev/c/Ef1V6RWoX1A/m/i7QjDE9bBAAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

1. The overall answer is to fool it.  Let eta = predicted value from the gbm model, then
fit a Cox model using eta.  You are simply getting the "setup" right so that the
downstream coxph functions will do the  computational work of the hazard function for you.

	eta <- predict(gbmfit, beth)  # beth = original data set for gbm fit
    	dummyfit <- coxph(Surv(time, status) ~ eta, init=1, iter=0, data= beth)

2. Get the expected probability at 5 years for each subject of interest.
         psurv <- survfit(dummyfit, newdata= beth2, se.fit=FALSE)
	event5 <- 1- summary(psurv, times=5)$surv    #probability of an event by time 5

Notes:
a. se.fit=FALSE simply speeds things up, in the case that you are not going to use the se
anyway.

b. I much prefer using newdata, with multiple rows, to tell the program which survivals
you want. It is more likely to remember the correct formula than I am.  It is also worth
getting used to this approach since it will do the correct variance for every subject,
which the "baseline" approach can't do.  In this particular case the se would not be
correct, however, since it needs the variance matrix of the original fit.  The variance of
eta will be too low, leading an underestimate.

c. The beth2 data set needs only one variable: eta.  To get the value of "eta" for
hypothetical new subjects, you of course need to use predict(gbmfit) again.

d. The next release of gbm will include (I hope) the ability for the Cox model to have
strata.  In that
case a strata() term also needs to appear in dummyfit.

Terry

PS: gbm folks: I thought this might be a useful note to tuck away.  User's will ask the
same question.

